course: dictionary
lesson: 15641
title: Terms: rules, relationships and words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word terms has more than one meaning, so the context tells you which one is meant. «Read the terms before you agree.» Here terms means the rules or conditions of an agreement.
explain: «Jack and Sam are on good terms again.» Terms does not mean rules here. On good terms means the two boys get on well with each other. Look at the words around the word to choose the right meaning.
explain: «In terms of size, the blue whale is the biggest animal.» In terms of means when you think about one particular thing. Test your meaning in the sentence: "When you think about size, the blue whale is biggest" fits, so you have it right.
explain: Terms can also mean special words used in a subject, like «maths terms» or «school terms». Be careful online too: the terms and conditions of an app are rules you agree to, so ask an adult before you click agree.
fact: Terms means the conditions or rules of an agreement, as in «the terms of the deal».
fact: «On good terms» or «on bad terms» describes how well people get on with each other.
fact: Terms is also the plural of term: school terms, maths terms and special words.
fact: «In terms of» means when you think about a particular thing, as in «in terms of size».
fact: «Terms and conditions» are the rules you agree to when you sign up for an app or buy something, so ask an adult first.
exercise: «Read the terms before you agree.» What do terms mean here? | *The rules or conditions of an agreement | The end of a school day | Special words used in maths | Friends who sit together
exercise: «Jack and Sam are on good terms again.» What does on good terms mean? | They are always shouting | They have signed an agreement | *They get on well with each other | They are in the same school term
exercise: «In terms of size, the blue whale is the biggest animal.» What does in terms of mean? | *When you think about one particular thing | Every single time | In the middle of a sentence | Because of the weather
test: «The class learned new maths terms today.» What are maths terms? | Friends who sit together | Rules you agree to when you sign up | *Special words used in maths | Times of the day
test: «Lily and her brother are not on speaking terms.» What does this tell you? | They have signed a deal together | They are talking all the time | They are the same age | *They are not talking to each other
exam: What should you do before you click «I agree» on an app's terms and conditions? | Click agree quickly so you can start | *Ask an adult to read them with you first | Share your password with a friend | Skip them because they are always the same
